    "body": "There is one test I end up failing when I fully remove all \"children changed\" code paths and I'm not sure how to make it pass without them. From https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/master/html/semantics/forms/the-select-element/inserted-or-removed.html it's this bit:\n```\ntest(() => {\n  const target = document.querySelector(\"#by-dom\");\n  // ...\n  const optgroup = document.createElement('optgroup');\n  const option3 = document.createElement('option');\n  option3.defaultSelected = true;\n  option3.textContent = 'First';\n  const option4 = document.createElement('option');\n  option4.defaultSelected = true;\n  option4.textContent = 'Second';\n  optgroup.appendChild(option3);\n  optgroup.appendChild(option4);\n  target.appendChild(optgroup);\n  assert_equals(target.selectedOptions[0].textContent, 'Second');\n}, 'The last selected OPTION should win; Inserted by DOM API');\n```\nThe problem is that when we run the insertion callback for _option3_, we mark that as the selected option and therefore _option4_ gets deselected because it's already in the tree at that point. And you can't fix this by having insertion behavior for `optgroup` elements, because when that callback runs, the callback for the `option` elements hasn't run yet and they won't have a `select` element owner yet.\n\nFor now in https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/pull/51940 I've decided to not regress this test by keeping our existing \"children changed\" code, but curious to hear what people make of this. Something probably has to give.\n\ncc @rniwa ",
